course: world
lesson: 3
title: The Moon and its phases
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Moon is a ball of rock that goes around the Earth about once a month; it makes no light of its own and shines only because sunlight falls on it.
explain: The Sun always lights one half of the Moon, but as the Moon goes around us we see different amounts of that lit half: these changing shapes are the phases.
explain: When the Moon is between the Earth and the Sun, its lit half faces away from us and we see a new moon; when the Moon is on the far side of the Earth from the Sun, we see the whole lit half, a full moon.
explain: In between we see a thin crescent, then a half moon, then a fat gibbous moon, growing (waxing) to full and then shrinking (waning) back to new, in about 29 and a half days.
explain: The phases are not made by the Earth's shadow: hold a ball at arm's length near a lamp, turn slowly around, and you will see all the phases on the ball yourself.
fact: The Moon goes around the Earth.
fact: The Moon makes no light of its own; it reflects the light of the Sun.
fact: The Sun always lights one half of the Moon.
fact: The phases show how much of the lit half of the Moon we can see from the Earth.
fact: From one new moon to the next takes about 29 and a half days.
fact: At new moon the Moon is between the Earth and the Sun, and its dark side faces us.
fact: At full moon the Earth is between the Sun and the Moon, and we see the whole lit side.
fact: A waxing moon grows bigger each night, and a waning moon grows smaller.
fact: The phases are not caused by the shadow of the Earth.
fact: The Moon is about a quarter as wide as the Earth.
fact: The Moon has no air and no liquid water, and it is covered in craters, round holes made by rocks from space crashing into it; with no wind or rain, the marks stay for ages.
fact: The full moon rises in the east around sunset.
exercise: Why does the Moon shine? | It reflects light from the Sun | It burns like a small star | It glows because it is hot | It reflects light from the Earth's cities
exercise: What goes around the Earth about once a month? | The Moon | The Sun itself | Mars, the red planet | The stars
exercise: What are the phases of the Moon? | The changing amounts of its lit half that we see | The changing size of the Moon itself as it swells and shrinks | Pieces of the Moon covered by clouds | The Earth's shadow moving across the Moon
exercise: Where is the Moon at new moon? | Between the Earth and the Sun | On the far side of the Earth from the Sun | Behind the Sun | Inside the Earth's shadow
exercise: Where is the Moon at full moon? | On the far side of the Earth from the Sun | Between the Earth and the Sun, in a straight line | Right next to the Sun in the sky | Far above the North Pole
exercise: How long is it from one full moon to the next? | About 29 and a half days | About 7 days, a single week | About 24 hours, a single day | About 365 days, a whole year
exercise: What does a waxing moon do? | It grows bigger night by night | It grows smaller night by night | It stays the same shape | It disappears for a week
exercise: Why can we not see the new moon? | Its lit side faces away from us | It is hidden by the Earth's shadow | It goes out for a night | It moves behind the Sun
exercise: What causes the phases of the Moon? | The Moon moving around the Earth so we see more or less of its sunlit half | The shadow of the Earth falling on the Moon | Clouds covering parts of the Moon | The Moon turning its light on and off
exercise: How big is the Moon compared with the Earth? | About a quarter as wide | About the same size | About twice as wide | About a hundred times smaller
exercise: Why does the Moon have so many craters? | Rocks from space hit it, and with no air or rain the marks stay | Volcanoes still erupt all over it | Giant animals dug holes in it long ago | Rivers carved them out
exercise: How much of the Moon does the Sun light at any time? | Always about one half | Only the part we see | All of it at once | A different amount each night
exercise: What phase comes after the full moon? | A waning gibbous moon | A new moon, straight away | A waxing crescent | A waxing half moon
exercise: When does a full moon rise? | Around sunset | Around sunrise | At noon, midday | At midnight exactly
test: Tom stands in a dark room with a lamp and holds a ball at arm's length, turning slowly around. What does he see? | The lit part of the ball grows and shrinks like the Moon's phases | The ball is always fully lit | The ball is always fully dark | The ball changes colour
test: Which is true? | The Moon's phases happen because we see its sunlit half from different angles | The Earth's shadow makes the phases each month | The Moon is a different size each night | The Moon makes its own light, which flickers
test: If the Moon stopped moving around the Earth, what would happen to its phases? | They would stop changing | They would change much faster | They would go backwards | Nothing would happen to them
test: Mia sees a thin crescent moon that is a bit bigger each evening. What will she see in a few more days? | A half moon | A new moon | The same crescent | A full moon at once
test: At full moon, which body is in the middle? | The Earth | The Moon itself | The Sun, as always | None of them
test: Why does the Moon look bright although it is made of grey rock? | Sunlight falls on it and bounces to us | It is covered in mirrors of shiny ice that sparkle | It is very hot inside | It is lit by the stars
test: How many full moons are there in a year? | About twelve or thirteen | About fifty, one each week | About four | Exactly one
test: A friend says a planet's shadow makes the Moon's phases. What is wrong? | The phases come from where the Moon is, not from any shadow | Nothing; a planet's shadow makes them | It is the Sun's shadow, not a planet's | It is the shadow of the clouds
test: Where would you look for a full moon just after the Sun has set? | Low in the east | Low in the west | High overhead | Low in the north
test: how come the moon's only half there tonight | We can see only part of its lit half from here | The Earth's shadow is covering the other half | A cloud is hiding the other half of it | The Moon has broken into pieces this week
test: moon's full tonight, when's it gonna be full again roughly | In about a month, around 29 and a half days | In about a week, around 7 days | Tomorrow night again, because it's full every single night | In about a year, around 365 days
test: my brother says the moon's got phases cos the Earth's shadow falls on it. true? | No, we just see different amounts of its sunlit half | Yes, the Earth's shadow makes every phase | Yes, but only the full moon is from the shadow | No, clouds on the Moon make the phases
test: Mia reckons the moon makes its own light like a lamp. right? | No, it only reflects light from the Sun | Yes, it glows like a small star | Yes, but only when it is full | No, it reflects light from the Earth's cities
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the source of the Moon's luminosity? | The Moon reflects the light of the Sun | The Moon emits light of its own production | The Moon reflects the light of the Earth's cities | The Moon is illuminated by the nearer stars
exam: Identify the principal cause of the phases of the Moon. | The varying portion of its sunlit half visible from the Earth | The shadow of the Earth falling upon the Moon | The interposition of clouds between Earth and Moon | The alteration of the Moon's actual shape
exam: State the approximate interval elapsing between one new moon and the subsequent new moon. | Approximately twenty-nine and a half days | Precisely seven days | Approximately three hundred and sixty-five days | Approximately fourteen days
exam: Whereas the Earth is situated between the Sun and the Moon, ascertain the phase thereby observed. | Full moon | A new moon | First quarter | Waning crescent
